WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.279
Anyone who gets to play Spider-Man is living
a dream come true.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:07.600
Unfortunately, for Andrew Garfield, that dream
was brief.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.320
Why was his tenure as Peter Parker so short?

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.360
The answer, as always, is complicated.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.469
Unwilling to compromise

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:17.740
The best characters often come from actors
with a concrete vision, who can turn stories,

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.790
experiences, and research into a coherent
whole.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:24.900
Garfield believed in the power of this process
as fervently as a 25-year-old rookie actor

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:25.900
could.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.360
After the series' cancellation, he told The
Guardian about the work he'd put in behind

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.330
the scenes to do right by the character:

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.140
“I thought, if I can infuse all this ancient
knowledge and wisdom into [Spider-Man], it

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.190
could be profoundly affecting for young people
in the audience.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.960
That was always my intention and what I tried
to do.”

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.620
Unfortunately, the studio didn't agree.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.910
The cutting of character-centric scenes, the
promotion of Garfield as a celebrity, and

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.609
the ultimate reality of The Amazing Spider-Man
as a blockbuster series made Garfield's high-brow

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:53.839
ideas unsustainable.

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:58.430
He has, at least, made piece with this rift,
telling The Guardian in the same interview:

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.109
"What I'll proudly say is that I didn't compromise
who I was, I was only ever myself."

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:04.239
Aiming higher

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.579
Andrew Garfield is an actor's actor, and that
means he was never going to be satisfied working

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:11.150
entirely in superhero blockbusters.

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.730
Ever since Tom Holland took up the webbed
suit to flip and quip his way through the

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:19.870
MCU's sprawling saga, Garfield has devoted
himself almost entirely to cerebral, arthouse

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:20.870
cinema.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:25.360
2016 saw him take part in Martin Scorsese's
Silence, in which he played a 17th-century

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.490
Jesuit priest.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.550
In 2017, he played a Polio survivor in the
biopic Breathe.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:34.930
Most recently, he starred in Under the Silver
Lake, a neo-noir that takes his character

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.570
across Los Angeles in pursuit of conspiracy.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.920
If the end of Spider-Man pushed Garfield into
this kind of work, its as much a blessing

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.649
as it is a curse - and perhaps Garfield himself
feels the same way.

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:46.770
A little too cool

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.939
Peter Parker is a consummate nerd.

00:01:48.939 --> 00:01:53.719
In his debut in Amazing Fantasy #15, he's
bitten by a radioactive spider because he's

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:55.780
visiting a lab after school.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.429
Peter Parker just loves science so dearly
that he likes to spend his time discussing

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:01.390
the finer points of atomic energy.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.759
Christened "Midtown High's only professional
wallflower" on the very first panel he ever

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:10.190
appeared in, he's a geek before he ever gets
the power to climb walls and swing across

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.190
New York.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.170
Sure, The Amazing Spider-Man movies inform
us of Peter's nerdery, but what we actually

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:16.520
see looks very different.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.319
Of the three actors who make up Spidey's big-screen
presence, Garfield is the one whose handsome

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:31.819
looks weren't toned down at all.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:36.769
Garfield's Peter Parker skateboards and broods
with a charisma that's entirely too cool.

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:40.370
Maguire's Spidey loved to talk chemistry,
and Holland's gets together with his best

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.290
friend Ned to build LEGO models of the Death
Star.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.370
But you can't really picture Garfield's Spider-Man
doing much in the geek arena beyond maybe

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.810
playing the occasional round of Mortal Kombat.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:54.150
He's just too cool, and when it comes to Spider-Man,
that's a fatal flaw.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.620
Competing with the MCU

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.280
The MCU debuted in 2008 with the explosive
arrival of Robert Downey Jr.'s Tony Stark

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.500
in Iron Man.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.480
By 2012, Marvel Studios had pulled its team
together in The Avengers, a blockbuster event

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.690
that would usher in what we might just one
day call the "Marvel Age" of movies.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.739
And, oh yeah, Spider-Man was there too.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:16.930
Of course, with Sony holding the movie rights
to the character, he remained trapped in his

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.519
own cinematic universe.

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:21.950
Though Amazing Spider-Man did well at the
box office, the fact remained that it was

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.730
completely eclipsed by the other Marvel-based
movie to come out that year.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:29.720
Plus, the MCU was demonstrating far more staying
power than Sony's spider-flicks.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:34.750
In retrospect, the writing was very much on
the wall: people were loving Marvel's sunny,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.790
quip-laden vision of their own universe far
more than with Sony's slick take on the iconic

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:39.870
webslinger.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.160
As the years went on, Marvel built up more
and more gravity and Spider-Man, the company's

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.569
flagship comic book hero, was pulled in and
revamped by Tom Holland.

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:52.730
There was an inevitability to this process
not even the best movie couldn't have thwarted…

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.680
and unfortunately, Garfield's films were far
from the best.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:56.730
Following Tobey Maguire

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.730
Director Sam Raimi's spin on Spider-Man feels
like ancient history now.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:05.219
But try not to let memories of Tobey Maguire
dancing in the jazz club cloud your mind just

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:06.219
yet.

00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:11.030
Instead, remember Alfred Molina's trailblazing
performance as a villain at war with himself.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.329
Remember Willem Dafoe's lunatic glee at being
able to bombard Spider-Man with pumpkin-themed

00:04:15.329 --> 00:04:16.329
bombs.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.759
"Are you in or are you out?"

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:21.720
"You're the one who's out, Gobby.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.030
Out of your mind!"

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.139
Remember Kirsten Dunst's warmhearted girl-next-door.

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:31.009
Remember the upside-down kiss, and perhaps
above all, remember that iconic train rescue.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:34.210
At the time, the world loved those movies.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:38.740
Only five years separate the last of the Maguire
trilogy from the first of the Garfield duo.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.870
In some ways, that's a long time.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.650
But it's not long enough for a generation
to turn over.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:48.550
Most kids who grew up with Maguire were barely
into adulthood by the time Garfield rolled

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.550
around.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.240
Maguire remained an A-list star, rather than
an elder statesman passing down a beloved

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:54.240
role.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.300
It was too soon for anyone to erase the mark
left by the first trilogy from their minds,

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.370
too soon for there to be an organic desire
for a new take on an old favorite.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.530
And in the end, that's the desire a superhero
flick lives and dies by.

